.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt ABSD-SH 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm absd-sh
.Nd the ABSD command shell
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c Ar line
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads one line at a time and runs it as one pipeline.
Without arguments it writes the prompt
.Ql absd$\ \&
before every line, and at the end of its input exits with the status of the
last line.
With
.Fl c
it runs
.Ar line
and exits with its status.
It is the shell an interactive SSH session starts.
It is deliberately small: it is not a POSIX shell and does not run
scripts.
.Pp
A command given to
.Nm ssh
.Pq Ql ssh host ls /bin
does not run under
.Nm :
the SSH server splits it at whitespace, with no quoting, pipes, or
redirection, and runs its first word directly (a bare name as
.Pa /bin/ Ns Ar name ) .
To run a pipeline without a terminal, write the lines to the shell's
standard input:
.Ql echo 'ls /bin | wc -l' | ssh host .
.Ss Words
A line splits into words at whitespace.
Double quotes group:
.Ql \&"a b"
is one word, and
.Ql a"b c"
is the word
.Ql ab c .
Inside quotes,
.Ql \e"
and
.Ql \e\e
stand for
.Ql \&"
and
.Ql \e ,
and
.Ql | ,
.Ql < ,
.Ql >
are ordinary characters.
Outside quotes a backslash is an ordinary character.
Nothing else is special:
.Ql $ ,
.Ql * ,
.Ql \(ti ,
.Ql \&; ,
.Ql & ,
and
.Ql #
are ordinary characters, so there are no variables, no globbing, no command
lists, and no comments.
An unterminated quote is a syntax error.
.Ss Commands
The first word names the command.
The builtins are listed below.
Any other name runs a program from the filesystem: the word itself when it
contains a
.Ql / ,
resolved against the working directory, otherwise
.Pa /bin/ Ns Ar name .
There is no
.Ev PATH
search.
A program is a static ABSD executable; there is no
.Ql #!
and no execute permission.
The remaining words are its arguments.
It starts with the shell's working directory and environment, and with any
of its three standard streams that is not piped or redirected shared with
the shell.
.Ss Pipelines and redirection
Commands joined by
.Ql |
run at the same time, each one's standard output feeding the next one's
standard input through a kernel pipe.
When a reader ends first, the writer's next write fails with a broken pipe
error; there is no signal.
A pipeline's status is its last command's.
.Pp
Each command may redirect its streams:
.Bl -tag -width "2>> file" -compact
.It Ic < Ar file
standard input from
.Ar file
.It Ic > Ar file
standard output to
.Ar file ,
created or truncated
.It Ic >> Ar file
standard output appended to
.Ar file
.It Ic 2> Ar file
standard error to
.Ar file
.It Ic 2>> Ar file
standard error appended to
.Ar file
.El
.Pp
The shell opens every redirection itself, with its own authority, before
anything starts; if one cannot be opened nothing starts and the status is
1.
The command never holds the file: its stream is a pipe, and the shell copies
between the pipe and the file.
.Pp
These are syntax errors (status 2, nothing starts): an empty command in a
pipeline, a redirection without a file name, the same stream redirected
twice, output both to a file and into a pipe, input both from a file and
from a pipe, and descriptor duplication such as
.Ql 2>&1 .
.Ss Narrowing a command's authority
By default a started command gets the shell's filesystem authority: a file
server channel bound to the shell's own mounts, never more.
Two prefixes narrow it for one command:
.Bl -tag -width "nofs cmd" -compact
.It Ic ro Ar command ...
the same mounts, all read-only
.It Ic nofs Ar command ...
no filesystem at all
.El
.Pp
A command started this way still writes its standard output and error;
.Ql ro cmd > file
writes
.Ar file
through the shell, while
.Ar cmd
itself cannot write anywhere.
The prefixes apply to programs, not to builtins.
.Pp
Each command needs a file server channel of its own: one of the shell's
spare channels (at most two, fewer while other sessions are open), or, for
one command at a time, the shell's own channel lent to it.
.Ic ro
and
.Ic nofs
need a spare, and so does every command while the shell is copying a
redirection.
A command that gets no channel is not started (status 126).
See
.Sx CAVEATS .
.Ss Builtins
.Bl -tag -width "shutdown"
.It Ic cd Op Ar dir
Change the working directory to
.Ar dir ,
which must be an existing directory the session can see; without
.Ar dir ,
to
.Pa / .
.It Ic pwd
Print the working directory.
.It Ic echo Op Ar word ...
Print the words separated by single spaces.
.It Ic status
Print the status of the last line.
.It Ic exit Op Ar n
Exit with status
.Ar n ,
or with the last status.
.It Ic ps
List this session's processes; see
.Xr ps 1 .
.It Ic shutdown Op Fl h | Fl p | Fl r Ar now
Ask
.Nm sshd
to halt the machine, or with
.Fl r
to restart it; see
.Xr shutdown 8 .
.It Ic reboot
Ask
.Nm sshd
to restart the machine; see
.Xr reboot 8 .
.El
.Pp
.Ic ps ,
.Ic shutdown ,
and
.Ic reboot
are requests to the SSH server on the session's control channel, which
only a shell
.Nm sshd
started for an interactive session holds; any other
.Nm
answers
.Ql no session control channel
with status 1.
.Pp
.Ic pwd ,
.Ic echo ,
.Ic status ,
and
.Ic ps
may start a pipeline (the shell writes their output into the next command)
and may take
.Ic > ,
.Ic >> ,
and
.Ic 2> .
.Ic cd ,
.Ic exit ,
.Ic shutdown ,
and
.Ic reboot
cannot be part of a pipeline.
.Ss Diagnostics and status
When the last command of a line exits with a nonzero status the shell
reports
.Ql absd-sh: Ar name Ns : exit status Ar n .
The statuses the shell itself assigns:
.Bl -tag -width "128" -compact
.It 1
a redirection could not be opened or completed
.It 2
a syntax error, or a builtin used wrongly
.It 126
the program was found but could not be started
.It 127
the program was not found
.It 128
the program ended without an exit status (it faulted or was killed)
.It 130
the line was interrupted with ^C (nothing more is printed)
.El
.Ss On a terminal
With a terminal
.Pq Ql ssh -t ,
or plain
.Ql ssh host ,
the SSH server, not the shell, echoes and edits the line: DEL or BS erases
a character, ^U the line, and ^C discards the line being typed.
.Pp
^C while a line runs interrupts it.
The key never reaches the command's input: the SSH server tells the shell
on the session's control channel, and the shell kills every command of the
running pipeline with the process handle it holds, waits for them, and
prompts again; the line's status is 130.
What a command wrote to a redirection file before it was killed stays
there.
The shell looks for ^C between bounded waits on its commands, so it acts
within a few milliseconds of the SSH server passing it on.
A ^C typed at the prompt only discards the line; it does not interrupt the
next command.
A line holds at most 4095 bytes.
^D on an empty line ends input for the whole session: a command reading
standard input sees end of file, and so does the shell, which then exits.
Standard output and standard error arrive on the one screen, in the order
written.
The terminal type and window size are not used.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ cat /etc/motd | wc -l
1
absd$ ls /home/operator > /home/operator/listing
absd$ ro cp /etc/motd /home/operator/copy
cp: /etc/motd -> /home/operator/copy: ABSD filesystem is mounted read-only
absd-sh: cp: exit status 1
absd$ nofs df
df: this process has no file server channel (-2)
absd-sh: df: exit status 1
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr intro 1 ,
.Xr ps 1 ,
.Xr filesystem 7 ,
.Xr handles 7 ,
.Xr process 7 ,
.Xr reboot 8 ,
.Xr shutdown 8
.Sh CAVEATS
^C ends the commands the shell started, not what they started in turn:
there is no process-tree kill.
A command that itself started programs (another
.Nm ,
for instance) leaves them running when it is interrupted; they are adopted
by init, keep the session's input, output, and filesystem channel until
they end, and may take lines typed at the prompt.
.Xr ps 1
counts them.
Without a terminal
.Pq Ql ssh -T
there is no ^C.
.Pp
Ending the SSH connection ends the shell; its running commands lose their
standard streams and their filesystem authority, and run to their own end.
.Pp
How many commands of one pipeline can hold a file server channel at once
depends on how many sessions are open; there is no fixed number to rely on
beyond two.
.Pp
Standard input is shared with the shell, but lines the shell has already
read are not visible to a command.
.Sh BUGS
There are no globbing, variables, command lists
.Po
.Ql \&;
and
.Ql &&
.Pc ,
descriptor duplication, job control, here-documents, or scripts.
